      "slug": "problem-of-evil",
      "kicker": "Philosophy",
      "title": "The logical problem of evil",
      "claim": "The existence of evil is logically incompatible with an omnipotent, omniscient, wholly good God.",
      "body": `<details class='deck-details' open><summary class='deck-summary'>The logical triad</summary><div class='details-content'><p>The logical problem of evil argues that certain attributes of God cannot logically coexist with the reality of evil. If God is all-powerful, He could prevent evil. If God is all-good, He would want to prevent evil. Yet evil exists.</p></div></details><details class='deck-details'><summary class='deck-summary'>The formal argument</summary><div class='details-content'><p>J. L. Mackie formalized this in 1955 as an inconsistent triad. The propositions 'God is omnipotent', 'God is wholly good', and 'evil exists' cannot all be true together.</p><blockquote>“God is omnipotent; God is wholly good; and yet evil exists. There seems to be some contradiction between these three propositions, so that if any two of them were true the third would be false.”<cite>J. L. Mackie, Evil and Omnipotence (1955)</cite></blockquote></div></details><details class='deck-details'><summary class='deck-summary'>The free will defense</summary><div class='details-content'><p>While apologists (like Alvin Plantinga) have proposed the 'free will defence'—arguing that a world with free creatures is better than a world without—the logical problem remains a central challenge. It demonstrates that classical theism requires complex philosophical gymnastics to remain logically coherent.</p><p>Even Plantinga acknowledges that while his defense may defeat the <em>logical</em> problem, the <em>evidential</em> problem of evil remains a serious challenge to theism.</p></div></details><details class='deck-details'><summary class='deck-summary'>Unresolved issues</summary><div class='details-content'><ul><li><strong>Mysterianism:</strong> Some theologians retreat to claiming God's goodness is simply beyond human comprehension, which undermines the claim that God is 'wholly good' in any meaningful human sense.</li><li><strong>Soul-making:</strong> The idea that evil is necessary for spiritual growth (Irenaean theodicy) fails to account for seemingly pointless natural evils (e.g., animal suffering).</li></ul></div></details>`,
